ORDERED-REAL CERTIFICATE: 0<a<r<b, r^3=m3, r^4<m4<chord.
Fd,Gd positive: x>0 and each displayed numerator/denominator factor positive.
All four bracket differences are positive; continuity and strict increase
give unique u in (r,b), d in (a,r) by IVT.
A3=u^3-a^3>r^3-a^3>0 and B3=b^3-d^3>b^3-r^3>0;
therefore 0<wu=(r^3-a^3)/A3<1 and
0<wb=(r^3-d^3)/B3<1. Weights normalize by construction.
Singular identities plus A3,B3>0 give exact third/fourth moments.
BOUNDARY CERTIFICATE: at m4=r^4, both bracketing targets equal
F(r),G(r), hence u,d tend to r and weights tend to 1,0; delta_r.
For every feasible measure, set z=y^3>0: (z^(4/3))''
=(4/9)z^(-2/3)>0. Strict Jensen equality at m4=m3^(4/3)
forces z=m3 almost surely, hence the unique measure is delta_r.
At m4=chord, u tends to b, d tends to a; both mixtures become
(1-lambda)delta_a+lambda delta_b, lambda=(r^3-a^3)/(b^3-a^3).
The strict secant inequality for z^(4/3) on [a^3,b^3]
forces chord equality to have support only at endpoints;
the m3 equation fixes lambda uniquely.
If m3 tends to a^3 or b^3, every probability measure on [a,b]
with that third moment converges weakly to the endpoint Dirac:
mass outside epsilon-neighborhood is bounded by the third-moment
gap divided by its positive endpoint separation. No 0/0 substitution.
